# Error boundary / recovery card

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

When a card cannot load or its configuration is not valid, Home Assistant draws a small card in its place instead of leaving the dashboard blank. It tells an administrator that something is wrong and which card it is. This page explains what people see and how to help them.

This page owns that card (`hui-error-card`). It has no YAML type that a dashboard author chooses. Home Assistant creates it. For wording failures and offering a way back, see [Errors and recovery](../patterns/recovery.html).

This page covers:

- Error boundary / recovery card
  - `hui-error-card`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

A single-row card with:

1. An icon: a circle with an exclamation mark for an error, a triangle for a warning.
2. A title next to the icon. By default it reads “Configuration error”, or “Configuration warning” for a warning. A card can supply its own text.
3. A message under the title, shown only in the card editor preview.

The card tints its whole surface with the error or warning colour at 12%. The title is one line and is cut off with an ellipsis when it is too long.

Who sees what:

| Who | Icon | Title | Message |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| An administrator on the dashboard | Yes | Yes | No |
| Anyone else on the dashboard | Yes | No | No |
| Anyone in the card editor preview | Yes | Yes | Yes |

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-hui-error-card"><code>hui-error-card</code></h3>

Home Assistant fills the card’s settings itself: `error` (the title), `message` (the detail) and `severity`, which is `error` by default or `warning`. A dashboard author cannot place the card or pick its text from YAML, so the way to remove it is to fix the card it stands in for, in the card editor.

**What the Civica theme sets.** The icon and the tint use `error-color` for an error and `warning-color` for a warning. The theme sets `error-color` to #CA3535 in light mode and #F0C6C6 in dark mode, and `warning-color` to #8A6100 in light mode and #DED3B8 in dark mode. The corners use the theme’s square `ha-card-border-radius`.

**What stays as Home Assistant draws it.** The icons, the 12% tint, the spacing, the one-line title and the rule about who sees the title and message are all Home Assistant’s. The theme cannot show the message to people who are not administrators.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- A broken card does not stop the rest of the dashboard from loading.
- The colour follows the theme in light and dark modes.
- Only the icon is shown to most people, so a missing card can look like a decorative mark.
- The message that says what went wrong appears only in the editor preview.
- The title is one line and can be cut off.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Treat the card as a sign for the person who looks after the dashboard. Fix the card behind it rather than leave it, and check the dashboard as someone who is not an administrator. Where a section could be missing for them, add a plain sentence nearby, such as “Some cards are not showing. Ask the person who looks after this dashboard to check them.” Say what failed and who can fix it, and do not blame the reader.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.1.1 Non-text Content and 1.4.1 Use of Colour: people who are not administrators get only an icon in a tinted card. Write the meaning in words nearby, because the card gives them none.
- 1.4.3 and 1.4.11 Contrast: the title and icon sit on a 12% tint of the error or warning colour. Test them in your own dashboard, in light and dark modes.
- 3.3.1 Error Identification and 3.3.3 Error Suggestion: the message with the cause is shown only in the editor preview. Check it there.
- 4.1.3 Status Messages: the card does not announce itself. Test with a screen reader in your own setup.

